WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.873
Every person who signs in to your site has a user account, and

00:00:04.873 --> 00:00:07.435
a role decides what that person can do.

00:00:07.435 --> 00:00:12.048
This video shows how to add people, create roles and give them permissions,

00:00:12.048 --> 00:00:17.060
choose how names and pictures appear, and let people sign in with an emailed code.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.294
Not everyone should see or change everything.

00:00:25.294 --> 00:00:31.280
An editor writes pages, a supervisor manages a team, and a visitor only reads.

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:37.771
Giving each person a long list of individual permissions is slow and easy to get wrong.

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:42.710
A role is a named set of permissions that you give to many people at once.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:49.317
Screens that show only sign-in names, such as dana dot whitfield, are hard to read.

00:00:49.317 --> 00:00:53.660
People expect to see Dana Whitfield, ideally with a picture.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:56.712
Users and roles give you both:

00:00:56.712 --> 00:01:02.020
access that is simple to manage, and names and avatars that people recognize.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:12.530
A user is one person's account, with a sign-in name, an email address and a password.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.893
A role is a named set of permissions, such as Editor.

00:01:16.893 --> 00:01:21.730
A person can hold several roles and gets every permission of all of them.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:26.899
Settings choose how names are built, with a display name, a first and

00:01:26.899 --> 00:01:30.140
last name, and whether each person has an avatar.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.204
A role can also be picked on a content item, so that the

00:01:34.204 --> 00:01:36.770
item is limited to the people who hold it.

00:01:40.976 --> 00:01:43.197
Open Access Control, Users.

00:01:43.197 --> 00:01:48.044
Each row is one person, with the user name, the email address, and

00:01:48.044 --> 00:01:49.996
badges for their roles.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:54.948
Unconfirmed means the person has not yet confirmed their email address.

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:59.330
The Search box finds people, and the Role, Show and Sort menus narrow

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:00.790
and order the list.

00:02:01.242 --> 00:02:06.298
The Actions menu on a row has the other things you can do with that account:

00:02:06.298 --> 00:02:09.812
Change password, Disable, Confirm email and Delete.

00:02:14.012 --> 00:02:15.243
Click Add User.

00:02:15.243 --> 00:02:19.172
Type the User name the person signs in with, and their Email.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:24.070
Phone Number is optional, and must include the country code.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:29.750
For the Password, click Generate password, and Copy password to pass it on safely.

00:02:30.201 --> 00:02:32.518
Tick the Roles the person needs.

00:02:32.518 --> 00:02:34.775
Here Nadia Brooks is an Author.

00:02:34.775 --> 00:02:37.641
The roles decide what she can see and do.

00:02:38.095 --> 00:02:39.092
Click Save.

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:42.435
The new person is on the list, with the role badge.

00:02:46.635 --> 00:02:48.845
Open Access Control, Roles.

00:02:48.845 --> 00:02:53.533
The system roles are Administrator, Anonymous and Authenticated.

00:02:53.533 --> 00:02:59.695
Anonymous is every visitor who is not signed in, and Authenticated is everyone who is.

00:03:00.147 --> 00:03:05.257
Click Add Role, type a Name, such as Shift Lead, and a Description.

00:03:05.257 --> 00:03:07.777
Then click Save and Configure.

00:03:08.228 --> 00:03:10.620
The permissions are grouped by feature.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.011
Allow gives the permission to the role.

00:03:13.011 --> 00:03:16.679
Effective also shows the permissions it gets from a higher one.

00:03:16.679 --> 00:03:19.868
The Search permissions box finds a permission by name.

00:03:20.321 --> 00:03:25.039
Tick Access admin panel so that shift leads can use the admin, and save.

00:03:25.039 --> 00:03:27.641
Everyone who holds this role gets it.

00:03:31.835 --> 00:03:34.812
To give someone access, edit the person.

00:03:34.812 --> 00:03:37.595
Here it is Dana Whitfield, an editor.

00:03:38.045 --> 00:03:40.616
Tick Shift Lead in Roles, and save.

00:03:40.616 --> 00:03:44.815
Dana now holds both roles, and gets every permission of each.

00:03:49.051 --> 00:03:51.372
Open Settings, User Display Name.

00:03:51.372 --> 00:03:56.073
The Display name format decides how a person's name is built wherever the

00:03:56.073 --> 00:03:57.501
site shows a user.

00:03:57.953 --> 00:04:00.169
Choose First Middle Last name.

00:04:00.169 --> 00:04:04.725
Then set First name and Last name to Required, so every account must

00:04:04.725 --> 00:04:06.141
have a full name.

00:04:06.141 --> 00:04:09.403
Display name and Middle name stay at Don't use.

00:04:09.855 --> 00:04:10.957
Click Save.

00:04:10.957 --> 00:04:12.512
Now edit a person:

00:04:12.512 --> 00:04:16.207
the editor shows only the name boxes you turned on.

00:04:16.207 --> 00:04:18.995
Here are Dana's first and last names.

00:04:19.445 --> 00:04:23.866
In the user list, Dana Whitfield now appears by name, and so does

00:04:23.866 --> 00:04:27.725
the user menu and the author badge in the content lists.

00:04:31.927 --> 00:04:34.354
Open Settings, User Avatars.

00:04:34.354 --> 00:04:39.923
Require Avatar makes a picture mandatory before an account can be saved.

00:04:39.923 --> 00:04:43.707
Use default CSS styles keeps the built-in look.

00:04:44.161 --> 00:04:48.378
To give someone a picture, edit the person and use the Avatar box:

00:04:48.378 --> 00:04:51.541
Add media picks an image from the media library.

00:04:51.541 --> 00:04:54.411
It is shown to people who may manage media.

00:04:58.606 --> 00:05:02.904
With the Sign in with an email code feature on, the sign-in page

00:05:02.904 --> 00:05:05.666
offers Email me a sign-in code instead.

00:05:06.117 --> 00:05:11.028
The person types the email address of their account, and clicks Email me a code.

00:05:11.028 --> 00:05:15.654
They receive a six-digit code, which they type on the next page to sign in.

00:05:15.654 --> 00:05:19.367
Accounts with two-factor sign-in keep using their password.

00:05:23.567 --> 00:05:28.061
Create a role for each job, such as Agent, Supervisor and Manager, and

00:05:28.061 --> 00:05:30.427
give people the role of their job.

00:05:31.177 --> 00:05:35.477
Bring a new team member on board with one account and one role.

00:05:36.227 --> 00:05:41.557
Show full names on author badges and user pickers, instead of sign-in names.

00:05:42.307 --> 00:05:46.747
Let occasional users sign in with a code they receive by email, with

00:05:46.747 --> 00:05:48.427
no password to forget.

00:05:50.427 --> 00:05:54.567
Create a role for each job, give people their roles, and choose how

00:05:54.567 --> 00:05:56.382
names and pictures appear.

00:05:56.382 --> 00:06:00.522
Access stays simple to manage, and people see names they recognize.

00:06:00.522 --> 00:06:01.997
Thanks for watching.

